{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2210 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "1bdfbddbc0cc35a3ad46ea97020fc7384657e3da3c1653c52e938013c5f326f8" :edb/db-before-t 2209 :edb/db-after-t 2210 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535522 50 #inst "2026-10-09T01:45:27.887Z" 13194139535522 true] [17592186105380 1100 "Evaluate the surface integral\n$\\iint\\limits_{S} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{z-x+y^2}} \\: \\textrm{d}S$\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=1+x+y^2$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $-1 \\leq y \\leq 1$." 13194139535522 false] [17592186105380 1100 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{z-x+y^2}} \\: \\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=1+x+y^2$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $-1 \\leq y \\leq 1.$" 13194139535522 true] [17592186105380 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D$, the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(1+x+y^2\\right) = 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(1+x+y^2\\right) = 2y\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z - x + y^{2}}}dS &= ∬_(D)f(x, y, g(x, y))\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(1)/(\\sqrt{(1 + x + y^{2}) - x + y^{2}}) \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + 1^{2} + (2y)^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + 2y^{2}}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2 + 4y^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + 2y^{2}}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2(1 + 2y^{2})}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + 2y^{2}}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + 2y^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + 2y^{2}}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + 2y^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{2}dA \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}∬_(D)dA \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot Area(D) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot (1 - 0) \\cdot (1 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535522 false] [17592186105380 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(1+x+y^2\\right) = 1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(1+x+y^2\\right) = 2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z-x+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(1+x+{y}^{2})-x+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2(1+2{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535522 true] [17592186105381 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/assets/d8675cb0c59ba915cefe4b0bbe539f1e7e38b5a3ac2fae4bd2bb25e4de96e116.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{S} x^2y \\: \\textrm{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=\\sqrt2x-y$ and whose domain is a semicircle of radius $1$ centered at $O$ in the upper-half plane, as shown above." 13194139535522 true] [17592186105381 1100 "Evaluate the surface integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{S} x^2y \\: \\textrm{d}S$,\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=\\sqrt2x-y$ and whose domain is a semicircle of radius $1$ centered at $O$ in the upper-half plane, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1264/Source/Images/8fda75933a423952bc1fd00fcda06419.png)" 13194139535522 false] [17592186105381 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D$, the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\sqrt2x-y) = \\sqrt2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\sqrt2x-y) = -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)x^{2}ydS &= ∬_(D)f(x, y, g(x, y))\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x^{2}y \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + (\\sqrt{2})^{2} + (-1)^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x^{2}y \\cdot \\sqrt{4}dA \\\\\n&= 2∬_(D)x^{2}ydA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=r\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2∬_(D)x^{2}ydA &= 2∬_(Δ)f(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ)rdrdθ \\\\\n&= 2∬_(Δ)r^{2}\\cos^{2} θ \\cdot r\\sin θ \\cdot rdrdθ \\\\\n&= 2∬_(Δ)r^{4}\\cos^{2} θ\\sin θdrdθ \\\\\n&= 2∫_{0}^{π}\\cos^{2} θ\\sin θ∫_{0}^{1}r^{4}drdθ \\\\\n&= 2∫_{0}^{π}\\cos^{2} θ\\sin θ[\\frac{r^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1}dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}∫_{0}^{π}\\cos^{2} θ\\sin θdθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{15}[- \\cos^{3} θ]_{0}^{π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{15}(1 + 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535522 false] [17592186105381 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\sqrt2x-y) = \\sqrt2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\sqrt2x-y) = -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,[\\frac{{r}^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{[-{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535522 true] [17592186105382 1100 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=xz$, where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x^2-y$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1$.\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below.\n$AAAAAAA$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535522 false] [17592186105382 1100 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=xz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x^2-y$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066807/assets/e44b57bad88dcc1c13680f0e8f110696c6975b16c1d9553138fb6aeb4cadb8f9.png)\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535522 true] [17592186105382 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D$, the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^2-y) = 2x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2-y) = -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)f(x, y, z)dS &= ∬_(D)f(x, y, g(x, y))\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)f(x, y, g(x, y))\\sqrt{1 + (2x)^{2} + (-1)^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x(x^{2} - y)\\sqrt{2 + 4x^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{1}(x^{3} - xy)\\sqrt{2 + 4x^{2}}dydx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue} (x^3-xy) \\sqrt{2+4x^2}}$." 13194139535522 false] [17592186105382 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^2-y) = 2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2-y) = -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(2x)}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,x({x}^{2}-y)\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,({x}^{3}-xy)\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue} (x^3-xy) \\sqrt{2+4x^2}}.$" 13194139535522 true] [17592186105812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353586 13194139535522 true] [17592186105812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353593 13194139535522 true] [17592186105812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353600 13194139535522 true] [17592186105812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353607 13194139535522 true] [17592186105813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353516 13194139535522 true] [17592186105813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353523 13194139535522 true] [17592186105813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353537 13194139535522 true] [17592186105813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353565 13194139535522 true] [17592186105813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353579 13194139535522 true] [17592186105814 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353530 13194139535522 true] [17592186105814 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353544 13194139535522 true] [17592186105814 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353551 13194139535522 true] [17592186105814 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353558 13194139535522 true] [17592186105814 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353572 13194139535522 true] [17592186105989 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535522 true] [17592186105989 1100 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$∬_(S)(z)/(\\sqrt{1 + 2\\cos^{2} (x + y)})dS$\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z = \\sin (x + y)$ for $0 \\le x \\le π$ and $-x \\le y \\le x$." 13194139535522 false] [17592186105989 1100 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+2{cos}^{2}⁡(x+y)}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)$ for $0\\le x\\le \\pi $ and $-x\\le y\\le x.$" 13194139535522 true] [17592186105989 1145 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\